    pr_ref:  # 108f
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-11 while correcting the Go arm of measurement 0002. Verified live\non dev 1102e75bd3, not inherited.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. go.py derives a package's identifier from the LAST segment of its\nimport path (`alias = import_path.rsplit(\"/\", 1)[-1]`, go.py:422). Go's semantic\nimport versioning puts a `/vN` element at the end of every major version >= 2, so\nthe derived alias is the literal string `v2` / `v4` and the real package\nidentifier is never bound. Every call on a value from such a package then misses\n`import_aliases`, `_go_external_import_path` returns None, and the dst falls back\nto the `external` placeholder.\n\nPOSITIVE / NEGATIVE PAIR IN ONE FILE, ONE analyze_go RUN — this is the control,\nbecause a bare \"these calls go external\" observation cannot separate the alias\ndefect from ordinary unresolved-external behaviour:\n\n  package encode  (module github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2)\n    import \"github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2\"                  -> alias 'v2'\n    import \"github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2/modules/caddyhttp\" -> alias 'caddyhttp'\n\n    caddy.RegisterModule(Gzip{})\n        -> go:external:0-0:RegisterModule:unresolved            LOST\n    caddyhttp.RegisterHandler(Gzip{})\n        -> go:github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2/modules/caddyhttp:0-0:RegisterHandler:unresolved\n                                                                RESOLVED\n\nSame file, same package, same run. The DEEPER path resolves and the MODULE ROOT\ndoes not, which is the signature of the alias derivation rather than of anything\nabout the callee.\n\nReproduced independently on three third-party roots:\n  github.com/gin-gonic/gin      -> alias 'gin'   -> go:github.com/gin-gonic/gin:0-0:JSON   RESOLVED\n  github.com/labstack/echo/v4   -> alias 'v4'    -> go:external:0-0:JSON                   LOST\n  github.com/gofiber/fiber/v2   -> alias 'v2'    -> go:external:0-0:Listen                 LOST\nAn explicit alias (`import v4echo \".../echo/v4\"`) resolves normally, which\nconfirms the defect is in the DERIVATION and not in path handling.\n\nBLAST RADIUS IS THE GO CALL GRAPH, NOT THE IO-BOUNDARY MAP. This drops the module\nhint for ANY call on a value from a /vN package, catalogued or not. Since Go\nmodules, `/vN` is the standard for every library at major version 2 or higher, so\nthe exposed population is a large and unmeasured share of real third-party Go.\n\nLIKELY ROOT CAUSE OF WI-tivij-putah-husuh-bulov-hagij-gopiv-jikur-lizat, WHICH IS\nFILED AS \"investigate ... alias imports?\" AND NOW HAS A NAMED MECHANISM. That item\nreports that on real Caddy a cross-package call to an IN-TREE function sometimes\nresolves to an external_symbol stub, breaking the init -> RegisterModule ->\nModule.CaddyModule chain, with 16 of 136 marker methods left unreachable. Caddy's\nmodule path is `github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2`, and the fixture above is\nmodelled directly on that item's own example (`gzip.go`'s\n`caddy.RegisterModule(...)`). NOT CLAIMED: that this accounts for all 16. If every\nregistration went through the root package all 136 would fail, so either the\nWI-zizig linker masks most of them or a second mechanism is present. Checking the\n16 against real Caddy is the next step and was NOT done here.\n\nDIRECTION: ADDS edges. Binding the correct alias resolves calls that currently go\nto `external`, so this is the recall direction, not the deleting one. It is not\nfree of hazard: newly-resolved calls re-enter name resolution, and the prior Go\ninitializer-typing attempt hijacked 118 of 692 moved edges (17.1%) onto the WRONG\nin-repo symbol (CHANGELOG.md:244). Ship with a hijack count in that shape.\n\nFIX SHAPE, unverified: strip a trailing `/vN` element (N >= 2, digits only) before\ntaking the last segment, per Go's own semantic-import-versioning rule. Guard the\ndegenerate case of a package legitimately named `v2` — the rule is that the suffix\nis only a version when it follows another element. A parity question worth asking\nbefore building: every language that derives an identifier from a path segment has\nthis shape of defect available, and no other analyzer was checked."  # 108f
    fields:  # 108f
      statement: "A package imported at a path ending in a Go semantic-version element (/v2, /v4, ...) must bind its real package identifier, so that calls on it carry the import path rather than the 'external' placeholder."  # 108f
      root_cause: "go.py:422 derives the alias as import_path.rsplit('/', 1)[-1], which for github.com/labstack/echo/v4 yields the literal 'v4'. The real identifier 'echo' is never registered in import_aliases, so _go_external_import_path (go.py:582-584) returns None and the dst falls back to go:external:0-0:<name>:unresolved."  # 108f
